#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171821 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5b00f0c-fc7f-5262-b1a3-cdd6c1560cd6" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Two-Dimensional Vector Field"}] :question/id #uuid "f0111439-4fd8-5add-b95f-5e783178fd63" :question/math-academy-id "e-9440" :question/problem "Draw a diagram that represents the vector field \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)= -2xy \\, \\mathbf{i} + x^2 \\, \\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n at the points $(-2,1), (-1,-2),$ and $(1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "To sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $\\mathbf F(x,y) = -2xy \\, \\mathbf{i} + x^2 \\, \\mathbf{j} \\,\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-2,1) & =-2(-2)(1)\\,i+(-2)^{2}\\,j=4\\,i+4\\,j \\\\ F(-1,-2) & =-2(-1)(-2)\\,i+(-1)^{2}\\,j=-4\\,i+j \\\\ F(1,-1) & =-2(1)(-1)\\,i+{1}^{2}\\,j=2\\,i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/cf51cba8548d1f342a9dd5c06b533782feb955fca4b90adfac5b6988d7e2e470.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171822 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9734fc0-9789-5a9c-8ae0-806f87052875" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Two-Dimensional Vector Field"}] :question/id #uuid "8f9615ef-b53e-51ee-9aa6-4df88afd6773" :question/math-academy-id "e-9441" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/2966d95bee0326ed5bf5a5fec9313d15bacd86334f9e9d144ff366bee63d9de3.png)\n\n \n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?\n\n- $\\mathbf F(x,y) = \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j}$\n\n- $\\mathbf G(x,y) = 2 \\, \\mathbf{i}$\n\n- $\\mathbf H(x,y) = -x \\, \\mathbf{i}$" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The vertical component (i.e., the $\\mathbf j$ component) is zero for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n- As we move away from the origin in the horizontal direction, our vectors' horizontal components (i.e., the $\\mathbf i$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf G(x,y) = 2 \\, \\mathbf{i}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf{H}(x,y) = -x\\, \\mathbf{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171826 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "888aa30d-7179-507c-a30c-38209587333b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Three-Dimensional Vector Field"}] :question/id #uuid "b226c391-9538-59ff-85ff-dde4d73f209b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9473" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/de305db47bcfae58b75797e88ae4a7c7825174bd58d77991ba8dfb4c0b8a13cb.png)\n\n \n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?\n\n- $\\mathbf  F(x,y,z) = \\mathbf j + \\mathbf k$\n\n- $\\mathbf  G(x,y,z) = \\mathbf k$\n\n- $\\mathbf  H(x,y,z) = z\\,\\mathbf k$\n\n- $\\mathbf  K(x,y,z) = -\\mathbf k$" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $z$-axis. So, the $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf j$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\mathbf j + \\mathbf k\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $z$-direction, so the $\\mathbf k$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf G(x,y,z) = \\mathbf k\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $\\mathbf i$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf H(x,y,z) = z\\,\\mathbf k\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf K(x,y,z) = -\\mathbf k.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172214 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "888aa30d-7179-507c-a30c-38209587333b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Three-Dimensional Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fa64aa7-5709-5a5b-8d86-fffb787a9837" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K(x, y, z) = e^{x}i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168488} {:answer/id #uuid "bbde0821-5ca8-5b14-9b26-42b2bd1117f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168489} {:answer/id #uuid "11e4e208-b631-5180-83d1-f903a669616e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H(x, y, z) = i + j + k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168490} {:answer/id #uuid "52cc7f27-742b-5dca-b965-b57b67eb0de6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "G(x, y, z) =-i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168491} {:answer/id #uuid "7fd9f1b2-9fe3-5b3b-a277-cd832859bb20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(x, y, z) = i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168492}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7fd9f1b2-9fe3-5b3b-a277-cd832859bb20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(x, y, z) = i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168492} :answer-field/id #uuid "902952fb-066e-5c9c-8874-81b4b024f4d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168487}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "505eb075-afb5-5225-b846-447773bcc89d" :question/math-academy-id "q-122559" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/cfc15b6202167211b7d6c0e8f6635dd9e571810d5296a209a4c317350604c4a4.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $x$-axis. So, the $j$ and $k$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nH(x,y,z)=i+j+k,\\,\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *positive* $x$-direction, so the $i$ component of our vector field is positive. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=-i\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $i$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y,z)={e}^{x}\\,i\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $F(x,y,z)=i.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319379 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9734fc0-9789-5a9c-8ae0-806f87052875" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Two-Dimensional Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b32f4a1b-fcd7-5355-abb2-a9fb831c67f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319381} {:answer/id #uuid "dc9a42a8-a975-50eb-8dbc-9af0ac7f48e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(x,y)=-i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319382} {:answer/id #uuid "30d20fde-59ea-5014-8c5f-3add4e03424b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "G(x,y)=i+2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319383} {:answer/id #uuid "8d0bd9a3-ba53-57c3-8e51-93594044106d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H(x,y)=2\\,i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319384} {:answer/id #uuid "484b5b70-110d-556d-a906-7e64bc023473" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K(x,y)=i+x\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319385}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "484b5b70-110d-556d-a906-7e64bc023473" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K(x,y)=i+x\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319385} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f217288-46b9-5277-b4d6-982695969913" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319380}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "116a3bdd-e190-5b6b-aeb5-2be696056404" :question/math-academy-id "q-122629" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/34aca579b2890586365298c764d051f57d4c80977ee975caad505c923343f90d.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $i$ component) is positive for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=-i+x\\,j\n$$\n\n- As we move away from the origin in the horizontal direction, our vectors' vertical components (i.e., the $j$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\nG(x,y)=i+2\\,j,\\,H(x,y)=2\\,i-j\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $K(x,y)=i+x\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319386 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9734fc0-9789-5a9c-8ae0-806f87052875" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Two-Dimensional Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1abe158-7fd1-56ed-911d-4147b9702dfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319388} {:answer/id #uuid "596c2222-0acd-56f0-ad79-571857ca5d52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H(x,y)=x\\,i-y\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319389} {:answer/id #uuid "8acd3898-ea09-5026-9d69-6adce1d50e47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(x,y)=-y\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319390} {:answer/id #uuid "fee57e9b-4be7-5728-aead-67e6e2b4b6c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "G(x,y)=-i+2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319391} {:answer/id #uuid "4c0f2cd0-23bd-5631-b2fb-0817a7e1448c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K(x,y)=-2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319392}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8acd3898-ea09-5026-9d69-6adce1d50e47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(x,y)=-y\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319390} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc053faf-eada-58af-bb4a-edd98ff400ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8feeeda9-af2b-54e8-bd0d-2099f4b0a0ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-122631" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/77f88ec1dfbd85b4609800484963d2480e68009eb4ac1f6c9fb620914633ff2a.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $i$ component) is zero for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\nG(x,y)=-i+2\\,j,\\,H(x,y)=x\\,i-y\\,j\n$$\n\n- As we move away from the origin in the vertical direction, our vectors' vertical components (i.e., the $j$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y)=-2\\,j\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $F(x,y)=-y\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319393 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9734fc0-9789-5a9c-8ae0-806f87052875" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Two-Dimensional Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b28620a7-93e3-52a8-9846-3b613df5948e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H(x,y)=x\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319395} {:answer/id #uuid "9dc56ab5-73f0-547d-ace7-aa129c58665a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "G(x,y)=5\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319396} {:answer/id #uuid "1953bc83-05d0-5634-b83b-3999bdd3928e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K(x,y)=-i-2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319397} {:answer/id #uuid "6cf47fdb-99ef-581b-8004-b514fb235785" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(x,y)=i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319398} {:answer/id #uuid "75b5461e-2593-5f0c-87bf-8f88fd71d649" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b28620a7-93e3-52a8-9846-3b613df5948e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H(x,y)=x\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319395} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a7bafc8-af95-5e0a-8b0a-a20717701803" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319394}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "57810971-964f-5e1b-92d0-da5475f2211a" :question/math-academy-id "q-122632" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/a032eb49f78ce7bd54de6969f030e4316f2bd3028280f73991af3fe45521733c.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $i$ component) is zero for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=i+j,\\,K(x,y)=-i-2\\,j\n$$\n\n- As we move away from the origin in the horizontal direction, our vectors' vertical components (i.e., the $j$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y)=5\\,j\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $H(x,y)=x\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319400 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9734fc0-9789-5a9c-8ae0-806f87052875" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Two-Dimensional Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f60f704f-c356-55dc-b588-c5706aa3e03c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(x,y)=-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319402} {:answer/id #uuid "19225c7c-d6e2-5a3d-a84d-67055462de3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K(x,y)=i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319403} {:answer/id #uuid "a9742b82-46d8-55c7-8986-61cf5551c289" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319404} {:answer/id #uuid "b5e13dc0-9a9c-52f9-87e2-f8223b807307" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H(x,y)=-i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319405} {:answer/id #uuid "7125cc6f-341a-54c0-9183-8c96b2236b02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "G(x,y)=j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19225c7c-d6e2-5a3d-a84d-67055462de3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K(x,y)=i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319403} :answer-field/id #uuid "f124e30a-955f-5bff-886e-4576bd7dc68c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319401}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d901bbc8-f64e-500c-b32f-04f048da371d" :question/math-academy-id "q-122634" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/d13f8c3fdea65c5f05df12585aec7e18564bdbe390ebb895b8b2d01dda74708a.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The vertical component (i.e., the $j$ component) is zero for each vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\nG(x,y)=j,\\,F(x,y)=-j\n$$\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $i$ component) is positive for each vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nH(x,y)=-i\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $K(x,y)=i.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319407 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9734fc0-9789-5a9c-8ae0-806f87052875" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Two-Dimensional Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9256e6c1-7130-50ad-9ca7-54acf6529fa7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319409} {:answer/id #uuid "064f54e3-a7bd-515c-9335-84211738f604" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H(x,y)=-5\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319410} {:answer/id #uuid "c8ba1668-70d9-50d1-a12f-fd877b5bf8be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K(x,y)=-3\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319411} {:answer/id #uuid "48b13a92-1d5b-5db8-a47b-5bcc0f7472e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(x,y)=4\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319412} {:answer/id #uuid "35b21cd5-2ce6-5d01-8468-196845ce2658" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "G(x,y)=2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319413}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "064f54e3-a7bd-515c-9335-84211738f604" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H(x,y)=-5\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319410} :answer-field/id #uuid "1879a797-565e-5247-af6c-c4899031ecf7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319408}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f783331a-9470-55ac-ba33-430f087c4abf" :question/math-academy-id "q-122637" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/721252c30370d98fe776ca00e43fef9c95a5a074140dfbdf2a00be19a66b4a1e.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $i$ component) is zero for each vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=4\\,i,\\,K(x,y)=-3\\,i\n$$\n\n- The vertical component (i.e., the $j$ component) is negative for each vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y)=2\\,j\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $H(x,y)=-5\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172216 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "888aa30d-7179-507c-a30c-38209587333b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Three-Dimensional Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "966275d1-af82-5a35-ad81-d04d70f7a2a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168500} {:answer/id #uuid "de6de102-1cb7-541a-ae2c-d752d13a95d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K(x, y, z) = 2k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168501} {:answer/id #uuid "122a8d26-22cb-54a8-bb65-2abd1f512055" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(x, y, z) = \\ln xk" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168502} {:answer/id #uuid "67c29821-3f10-56d9-96b3-60fedc981778" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "G(x, y, z) = i - k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168503} {:answer/id #uuid "67e5e9a4-cb82-59f9-b4b5-137039129695" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H(x, y, z) =-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de6de102-1cb7-541a-ae2c-d752d13a95d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K(x, y, z) = 2k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168501} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7830737-52c7-561f-a08d-87ca04053e88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168499}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0777fbfe-65b4-5afe-92ce-361fbbf5b37f" :question/math-academy-id "q-122664" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/7a0a919af689e9c332ba6ceb3d606208dab9eabadef9b4871ac8e164843ebe88.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $z$-axis. So, the $i$ and $j$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=i-k\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *positive* $z$-direction, so the $k$ component of our vector field is positive. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nH(x,y,z)=-k\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $k$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,k\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $K(x,y,z)=2\\,k.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319414 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "888aa30d-7179-507c-a30c-38209587333b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Three-Dimensional Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "154cb501-e311-5a15-a12b-1f321ed4235d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319416} {:answer/id #uuid "9ffb5fe6-c48c-5ddd-ae31-e45968b8893a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(x,y,z)=-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319417} {:answer/id #uuid "d2026499-1103-56f4-aa9f-8f22f24b0eae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K(x,y,z)=2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319418} {:answer/id #uuid "ac49ec6a-72b4-5eb1-90f3-b5460248a06b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "G(x,y,z)=4\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319419} {:answer/id #uuid "29076f44-e74e-5456-bb79-77103e057e29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H(x,y,z)=x\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319420}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ffb5fe6-c48c-5ddd-ae31-e45968b8893a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(x,y,z)=-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319417} :answer-field/id #uuid "b50a8900-b25b-5044-a1f2-edab660ccf91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319415}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bceb2084-6986-55be-bfe3-3edd2e31db29" :question/math-academy-id "q-122668" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/f89653698d9c2bad0c2e6a77d4dad1953a9fd72f89cbfc08fd68aac802acd284.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $y$-axis. So, the $i$ and $k$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=4\\,k\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $y$-direction, so the $j$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y,z)=2\\,j\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $j$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nH(x,y,z)=x\\,j\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $F(x,y,z)=-j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319421 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5b00f0c-fc7f-5262-b1a3-cdd6c1560cd6" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Two-Dimensional Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "317ccade-3188-5caa-b6ca-0cb5e34dd65b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/5e9e75b66969e1acd7b1a90a5243a3a496168644a53d541851ba933a5f465553.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319423} {:answer/id #uuid "a4953b1e-72bd-5965-9645-73dcd8e94d1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/0c69bb201b98cd3728527c562bf4e92cef3c48adae4e01725c2497a73c97a0af.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319424} {:answer/id #uuid "a0aaca2e-7217-5f87-8a53-337ffcc68281" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/2100cd71f6bbbcc46d3f9f35853f65a9bf0d3f25bf3273db0d244c05221d758d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319425} {:answer/id #uuid "d7e0ce32-f386-559d-a47b-91bc3e8514bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/34e44b959282b0bc6c245643329e2b485d3209d89f304cf76c106718120a2f10.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319426} {:answer/id #uuid "167d1faa-7cbf-56fe-b933-b01ef4fb2f5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/fc954607d17ae1eadaa0aba8d939343290a5c20f47bf61863cba9c42d9f558c9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319427}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "317ccade-3188-5caa-b6ca-0cb5e34dd65b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/5e9e75b66969e1acd7b1a90a5243a3a496168644a53d541851ba933a5f465553.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319423} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea079116-6174-5adf-86ff-c05e14ec3885" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319422}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a6e4754b-623a-592f-86c3-7bca425b2a5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-122742" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $F(x,y)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})\\,i+xy\\,j$ at the indicated points?" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-1,1),(1,2),$ and $(2,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $F(x,y)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})\\,i+xy\\,j\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-1,1) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,i+(-1)(1)\\,j=-3\\,i-j \\\\ F(1,2) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,i+(1)(2)\\,j=3\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ F(2,-1) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )\\,i+(2)(-1)\\,j=-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/5e9e75b66969e1acd7b1a90a5243a3a496168644a53d541851ba933a5f465553.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319428 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5b00f0c-fc7f-5262-b1a3-cdd6c1560cd6" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Two-Dimensional Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee4858c7-b062-560b-b3c6-89f462c2ea46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/68d33b11d6476a0d35cb7486073e90e2755b46f1d4f935adc5dafb7087b15b99.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319430} {:answer/id #uuid "b176047b-10ea-51fc-8f45-9dfc98bac27d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/58a106f2c0ac7aacd30f7ba8915fd2317a67efb73c1c48fe2d192a5d2576717a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319431} {:answer/id #uuid "7c58ad91-7773-5f13-a896-fbca668a974c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/e87092c467b78bfe00651fb9752ed54c7f7784c83612fba2149063eba57952e9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319432} {:answer/id #uuid "2c350286-1dcd-5c69-a727-75bfea8b5099" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/4837efc1a78060b2954fa58b26f35e7b2c6d810ce676959653f8e7eca0b5b476.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319433} {:answer/id #uuid "d03b273a-1d98-51eb-9b7a-75ccba050f8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/f2835e43d43494d3319a6a0db0a411906f0424bdac8830425bad47723010d95c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319434}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b176047b-10ea-51fc-8f45-9dfc98bac27d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/58a106f2c0ac7aacd30f7ba8915fd2317a67efb73c1c48fe2d192a5d2576717a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319431} :answer-field/id #uuid "22f1850c-0467-5560-a78a-973c20a2e2de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c4a8ac52-6bf2-589c-a450-918b551390b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-122744" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $F(x,y)=-2x\\,i+{2}^{y}\\,j$ at the indicated points?" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-4,2),(-1,1),$ and $(2,0).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $F(x,y)=-2x\\,i+{2}^{y}\\,j\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-4,2) & =-2(-4)\\,i+{2}^{2}\\,j=8\\,i+4\\,j \\\\ F(-1,1) & =-2(-1)\\,i+{2}^{1}\\,j=2\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ F(2,0) & =-2(2)\\,i+{2}^{0}\\,j=-4\\,i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/58a106f2c0ac7aacd30f7ba8915fd2317a67efb73c1c48fe2d192a5d2576717a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319435 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5b00f0c-fc7f-5262-b1a3-cdd6c1560cd6" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Two-Dimensional Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e6f1393-4bc8-55b9-b33e-39180408b5c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/d44395fa166c654c41322bd31e1a9bf9bc6c7a398e27da7f9de9d249ccdb1107.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319437} {:answer/id #uuid "54c254fb-1c77-5d0b-876a-c72ea02a3e70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/2560beb4dcff267890c47ad5df8ac7aef0e5de64a57384f8c71e35d8325c892b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319438} {:answer/id #uuid "bddef06e-dacf-51a4-bc95-65b31236fb10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/8cdc92839252560ad52b54bb77d9db75ba066e33bfa5a3c451e4cbb6345e9d33.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319439} {:answer/id #uuid "e43d7b14-ba50-5d45-bf7a-fdef1eaa0894" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/b6a7c1faba2bb5d18d5f729d2576d7475f6ce1784698fae0a1c1ea97b80d6180.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319440} {:answer/id #uuid "9dfb64a8-85e2-5106-98dc-2d8368832888" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/8ec9af02a374c1b8a954117ad055f78e9e2ac74bc391bb230968198a3a474fef.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319441}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9dfb64a8-85e2-5106-98dc-2d8368832888" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/8ec9af02a374c1b8a954117ad055f78e9e2ac74bc391bb230968198a3a474fef.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319441} :answer-field/id #uuid "acbc9dbd-8e27-50c2-9fe2-e3fcfcbc0325" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7e446a55-b0d3-59f4-abde-52af19baebe8" :question/math-academy-id "q-122745" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $F(x,y)=\\frac{y}{xy+1}\\,i+\\frac{x}{xy+1}\\,j$ at the indicated points?" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,0),(1,-2),$ and $(0,2).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $F(x,y)=\\frac{y}{xy+1}\\,i+\\frac{x}{xy+1}\\,j\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-2,0) & =\\frac{0}{(-2)(0)+1}\\,i+\\frac{-2}{(-2)(0)+1}\\,j=-2\\,j \\\\ F(1,-2) & =\\frac{-2}{(1)(-2)+1}\\,i+\\frac{1}{(1)(-2)+1}\\,j=2\\,i-j \\\\ F(0,2) & =\\frac{2}{(0)(2)+1}\\,i+\\frac{0}{(0)(2)+1}\\,j=2\\,i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/8ec9af02a374c1b8a954117ad055f78e9e2ac74bc391bb230968198a3a474fef.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319442 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5b00f0c-fc7f-5262-b1a3-cdd6c1560cd6" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Two-Dimensional Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5af108a7-b7dc-5640-a23d-0d81e0434fb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/040d62d956945b7bb67cae560cda4e09a22be2fa9cd2e5da3f3cc372269f14e4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319444} {:answer/id #uuid "488e8e7f-92f9-532c-86c8-7164362e7d1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/80ecb3a560bc23359c3b44baace0523bfe653f0c5a5fee5b5c646d901ade5feb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319445} {:answer/id #uuid "edd36297-0810-5ff8-80a8-27784d3f1fd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/d6084f0711f728a09a78c9803bec9c6e54274d6d72a2adc2b1547651e0ec702c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319446} {:answer/id #uuid "1e070062-8e49-5695-824f-b63676481c72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/4c9fc6ae3cbbf6fa3d241d3607b26fee1cd0670fde37494638ca90da2b02ecfa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319447} {:answer/id #uuid "5d53af34-82e4-5e80-8f4d-b45d54ddb9a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/3e9efea8998b1dbaab3cc6d89dce4f99bdb06066e81fcb78a2b3a0e412e2e44c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edd36297-0810-5ff8-80a8-27784d3f1fd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/d6084f0711f728a09a78c9803bec9c6e54274d6d72a2adc2b1547651e0ec702c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319446} :answer-field/id #uuid "89aadcd6-700b-5db5-a54c-45992b942778" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319443}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b5b3650-421e-53e6-b6b4-9b4c7a243072" :question/math-academy-id "q-122754" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $F(x,y)=(y-2)\\,i+(x+2)\\,j$ at the indicated points?" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-5,3),(0,0),$ and $(2,-3).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $F(x,y)=(y-2)\\,i+(x+2)\\,j\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-5,3) & =(3-2)\\,i+(-5+2)\\,j=i-3\\,j \\\\ F(0,0) & =(0-2)\\,i+(0+2)\\,j=-2\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ F(2,-3) & =(-3-2)\\,i+(2+2)\\,j=-5\\,i+4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/d6084f0711f728a09a78c9803bec9c6e54274d6d72a2adc2b1547651e0ec702c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319449 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5b00f0c-fc7f-5262-b1a3-cdd6c1560cd6" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Two-Dimensional Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c1ad204-c357-5677-88f4-f72f71cdb400" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/d150a7172d3a7e876cda685d9b096dd1f8d7d96a3349e355dc26562af75caa47.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319451} {:answer/id #uuid "d56dc921-f2e1-53bb-b5c5-6dbc673c12bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/81ec06546a0ae47f254f42bfafabcf10c6aa7b776f49c59013c9c198bf1bf89a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319452} {:answer/id #uuid "b7369ab5-e7dc-516e-855a-1878a29f04f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/e959a73a075acd0e72e4cfa4edd09fa1710c450286ff574574208d42b2d897c4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319453} {:answer/id #uuid "12fc4ce6-e1f2-54e9-85c9-554c852a771b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/330bf1e0b9b2dff008a3e3878d5b2acba8e14addb637d637874492220cb08f2c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319454} {:answer/id #uuid "559410b9-76ea-5479-94a2-72136b5aff78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/b014bb3efef5caa6a721173a7470f4600edc8207cae6adc3a77bffe217176e13.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319455}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12fc4ce6-e1f2-54e9-85c9-554c852a771b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/330bf1e0b9b2dff008a3e3878d5b2acba8e14addb637d637874492220cb08f2c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319454} :answer-field/id #uuid "6af54ff9-f4e7-57c4-9394-97ca172a1e79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319450}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a92ed993-56d7-5e64-a638-5c73e66c6e42" :question/math-academy-id "q-122759" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $F(x,y)=-\\frac{x}{y}\\,i-|x|\\,j$ at the indicated points?" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-3,1),(2,-1),$ and $(4,2).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $F(x,y)=-\\frac{x}{y}\\,i-|x|\\,j\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-3,1) & =-(\\frac{-3}{1})i-|-3|\\,j=3\\,i-3\\,j \\\\ F(2,-1) & =-(\\frac{2}{-1})i-|2|\\,j=2\\,i-2\\,j \\\\ F(4,2) & =-(\\frac{4}{2})i-|4|\\,j=-2\\,i-4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/330bf1e0b9b2dff008a3e3878d5b2acba8e14addb637d637874492220cb08f2c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319456 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "888aa30d-7179-507c-a30c-38209587333b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Three-Dimensional Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "519aef0d-7b37-512b-ab03-c32758e7dfa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319458} {:answer/id #uuid "1a456264-4d0f-5c10-a40f-543b1c656c18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K(x,y,z)=-i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319459} {:answer/id #uuid "6e77bd46-9d86-5c54-8594-0e660dd7b75a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "G(x,y,z)=-y\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319460} {:answer/id #uuid "d6658168-8da9-550f-918b-0e73a5cef352" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H(x,y,z)=-2\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319461} {:answer/id #uuid "e2a1d9e3-8be9-534d-9d07-4b52024990c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(x,y,z)=i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319462}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6658168-8da9-550f-918b-0e73a5cef352" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H(x,y,z)=-2\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319461} :answer-field/id #uuid "695d6c25-7df8-5948-81f9-6d74f471dbe8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319457}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "12faec5e-3cd9-5bb0-aed4-2ef29d9f5015" :question/math-academy-id "q-123748" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/bff60466ca8abba4fd849c046c02af3a4811279774975fc981cafad91d734ed5.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $x$-axis. So, the $j$ and $k$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y,z)=-i+j\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $x$-direction, so the $i$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=i\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $i$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=-y\\,i\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $H(x,y,z)=-2\\,i.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319463 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "888aa30d-7179-507c-a30c-38209587333b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Three-Dimensional Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "221bca38-d366-5c74-a0ff-4ab7cc0dc075" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319465} {:answer/id #uuid "a0e174e4-ecd5-5fff-89ab-16415fbc8931" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K(x,y,z)=i+{e}^{x}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319466} {:answer/id #uuid "1e627b35-0aef-54d7-a8fb-88b9e81bcd95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F(x,y,z)=j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319467} {:answer/id #uuid "b73b2517-7721-528a-b7d0-c4068b252d12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H(x,y,z)=i+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319468} {:answer/id #uuid "1cdec719-9241-5862-802b-f5977a170a00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "G(x,y,z)=-{x}^{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319469}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "221bca38-d366-5c74-a0ff-4ab7cc0dc075" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319465} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb019a3e-7c6d-51dc-b650-e8683c2a8aeb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319464}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "19f56bd4-7f58-5f33-a5af-fe988cedd30d" :question/math-academy-id "q-123749" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043096/assets/f89653698d9c2bad0c2e6a77d4dad1953a9fd72f89cbfc08fd68aac802acd284.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $y$-axis. So, the $i$ and $k$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\nH(x,y,z)=i+k,\\,K(x,y,z)=i+{e}^{x}\\,k\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $y$-direction, so the $j$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=j\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $j$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=-{x}^{2}\\,j\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram doesn't correspond to any of the listed vector fields."}]}
